# CMA-ES

The covariance matrix adaptation evolution strategy, CMA-ES, learns a
Gaussian search distribution from objective rankings. It adapts the
distribution's center, overall scale, and shape, including correlations
between parameters. Basin implements active CMA-ES, which also uses poorly
ranked samples to reduce exploration in unsuccessful directions.

[API
reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/cma_es/struct.CmaEs.html)

| Property | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| Objective | A scalar function of continuous parameters |
| Evaluations | `CostFunction` |
| Search | Stochastic population search from an initial mean |
| Constraints | Unconstrained; declarations are ignored |
| Parameters | All four dense backends, with `f32` or `f64` |

## When to use it

Consider CMA-ES when derivatives are unavailable and interactions between
parameters make independent coordinate steps ineffective. Learning a full
covariance can help on elongated valleys and problems whose useful search
directions do not align with the coordinate axes. [Hansen
(2016)](#ref-hansen-2016) derives the method and its default parameters.

CMA-ES spends multiple evaluations on each generation and stores a dense
covariance matrix. It suits small to moderate dimensions when those costs
are acceptable. If gradients are reliable, compare a local method such as
[L-BFGS](../../quasi-newton/l-bfgs/). For a population method without covariance
factorization, compare [differential evolution](../de/).

A single run can concentrate on one local optimum. A learned distribution
and a low objective value do not certify global optimality. Compare runs
with different seeds and starting means when finding different basins
matters. Use [bounded CMA-ES](../bounded-cma-es/) when the objective must
only receive points inside a box.

## How it works

The search distribution is `N(m, sigma² C)`, with mean `m`, scalar scale
`sigma`, and covariance shape `C`. Initialization uses `C = I`, or
`C = diag(stds²)` when configured with `with_stds`. The default population
size is `lambda = 4 + floor(3 ln n)` for `n` parameters.

Initialization draws and evaluates a population and evaluates its mean.
Each subsequent generation ranks the previous samples by increasing
objective value and moves the mean toward a weighted combination of the
best half. Two smoothed histories of the mean's motion, called evolution
paths, guide the scale and covariance updates. Persistent movement can
increase the scale, while short, inconsistent movement can decrease it.

The covariance update combines the selected samples and a history of
successful directions. Negative weights from poorly ranked samples make
the update active. Basin refreshes the symmetric eigendecomposition every
generation, then draws the next population from the updated distribution.

The current result reports the distribution mean and its evaluated cost.
The historical best considers both the sampled members and the mean, so
`best_param()` can differ from `param()`. Neither sequence of mean costs
nor the best member of each new generation must improve monotonically.

## Example

The sphere objective has its unique minimum at the origin. This example
starts with five coordinates equal to `1`, uses a fixed seed, and stops on
a small search distribution or the generation budget. The mean and the
best evaluated point both approach the minimum.

<!-- compile: solver-cma-es -->
```rust
use basin::{CmaEs, CostFunction, DenseMatrix, Executor};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct Sphere;

impl CostFunction for Sphere {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Output = f64;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn cost(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        Ok(x.iter().map(|v| v * v).sum())
    }
}

fn main() {
    let solver = CmaEs::<Vec<f64>, DenseMatrix>::new(7, 0.5)
        .with_absolute_distribution_size_tolerance(1e-10);

    let result = Executor::from_start(Sphere, solver, vec![1.0; 5])
        .max_iter(300)
        .run()
        .unwrap();

    assert!(result.cost() < 1e-6);
    assert!(result.best_cost() <= result.cost());
    assert!(result.best_param().iter().all(|v| v.abs() < 1e-3));
}
```

## Practical considerations

### Choose the initial scale and coordinate units

`CmaEs::new(seed, sigma)` requires a finite, strictly positive `sigma`.
It sets the initial standard deviation in every coordinate when the
covariance is isotropic. Match it to the size of the region you want to
explore around the initial mean.

Use `with_stds` when parameters have different natural scales. Each
coordinate initially has standard deviation `sigma * stds[i]`; these
values are relative factors, not replacements for `sigma`. The vector
must match the mean's dimension and contain finite, strictly positive
entries. Adaptation can learn correlations later, but useful initial
scaling can save many evaluations.

### Count generations and evaluations separately

`with_lambda` overrides the population size and requires at least four
members. A larger population spends more evaluations per update and can
improve exploration, so compare settings at a fixed evaluation budget.
`default_lambda(n)` exposes the default formula.

Initialization uses `lambda + 1` objective evaluations, and every completed
generation uses another `lambda + 1`. The extra evaluation measures the
mean, even when a sampled member has a lower cost. Executor budgets are
checked at iteration boundaries and can therefore be exceeded by work
inside a generation. Set a generation limit as well as any evaluation
limit needed for your application.

### Interpret distribution convergence carefully

There is no default convergence test. Configure
`with_absolute_distribution_size_tolerance` for TolX, which compares the
largest principal-axis standard deviation, `sigma * max(d[i])`, with the
tolerance. Here `d[i]` is the square root of a covariance eigenvalue.
`None` disables the check; zero requests exact collapse. A positive
threshold uses a strict inequality and reports `CmaEsTolerance`.

The tutorial suggests `1e-12 * initial_sigma` for isotropic starts; account
for the largest initial axis scale when using `with_stds`, and choose a
threshold appropriate for the scalar precision and parameter units. This
test describes the search distribution, not a gradient or an error bound.
Basin does not implement the tutorial's other CMA stopping heuristics,
including covariance-condition and fitness-stagnation tests.

### Keep the objective defined where sampling can reach

Gaussian sampling has unbounded support. Plain CMA-ES ignores constraint
declarations and does not repair points. Select bounded CMA-ES for box
bounds, or apply an explicit problem transformation suited to other
constraints. Sorting places NaN costs last, but that ordering does not
make an objective with mostly invalid samples a useful search problem.
A covariance eigendecomposition failure reports numerical failure.

## Implementation

`PopulationProgress` owns evaluated members, their costs, the evaluated
mean, and the incumbent. `Executor::from_start` creates it from an initial
mean; the explicit equivalent is `PopulationProgress::from_point(mean)`.
The population is sorted by objective cost. The solver owns the mean,
covariance, eigenpairs, evolution paths, scale, constants, and seeded
`ChaCha8Rng`.

Retain the solver with `Executor::run_with_solver` to inspect `mean()`,
`sigma()`, or the borrowed `distribution()` diagnostics. Reading those
views performs no evaluations. Dense covariance storage costs `O(n²)`,
population storage costs `O(lambda * n)`, and refreshing its decomposition
adds cubic dense matrix work per generation.

Fresh initialization restores the original seed and configured scale and
covariance, then evaluates a new population around the supplied point.
An exact solver-and-state checkpoint retains the live distribution, RNG,
population, and counts and skips initialization. A state-only snapshot
starts a new distribution. Repeated runs with the same seed and backend
reproduce a deterministic objective's trajectory; different backends can
round eigendecompositions differently.

Supported pairs are `Vec<F>`/`DenseMatrix<F>`,
`nalgebra::DVector<F>`/`DMatrix<F>`, `ndarray::Array1<F>`/`Array2<F>`, and
`faer::Col<F>`/`Mat<F>`, with `F = f32` or `f64`. Specify the matrix type on
the solver. All four dense paths provide the required symmetric
eigendecomposition in pure Rust, without a BLAS/LAPACK provider.

